Blitz3D sample files
--------------------

The files in examples/assets/blitz3d come from the samples of the Blitz3D
open source release (https://github.com/blitz-research/blitz3d,
_release/samples), Copyright (c) 2013 Blitz Research Ltd, under the same
zlib/libpng licence as the Blitz3D compiler above. Models among them were
converted to glTF for PolyBasic. examples/tron.pb is a port of the Tron
sample, which has no files of its own: its grid texture is drawn in code.
The castle demo's files (examples/assets/blitz3d/castle: the castle and the
runner as .glb made from their .x files with tools/x2gltf.mjs, the textures,
sky box, sprites and sounds) are the ones its sample comes with; the terrain
heightmap and ground texture are the driving sample's (the same files).
The teapot (examples/assets/blitz3d/teapot: teapot.glb made from teapot.x with
tools/x2gltf.mjs, and the sphere map picture) is the teapot sample's.
The dragon (examples/assets/blitz3d/dragon) is credited in its sample as
"MD2 Dragon model courtesy of Polycount"; the gargoyle
(examples/assets/blitz3d/gcuk) comes with Paul Gerfen's GCUK tutorials
(www.gamecoding.co.uk) in the same release.

The playground's page layout and scripts follow the DivJS playground
(https://github.com/akadjoker/divjs-playground), by the same author, MIT.


Fox model
---------

examples/assets/fox/fox.glb is the Fox of the Khronos glTF Sample Assets
(https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF-Sample-Assets/tree/main/Models/Fox):
model by PixelMannen (CC0 1.0); rigging and animation by tomkranis
(CC BY 4.0, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/); glTF conversion
by @AsoboStudio and @scurest (CC BY 4.0). fox-run.glb is made from it, with
only its joints and its Run animation.

